# Synopsys DC analysis DB + HAPS FPGA-prototype build outputs (not source)
/AN.DB/
# Ignore the BUILD OUTPUTS ONLY. These rules used to be /fpga/haps-sx/ and
# /fpga/haps-sx-pc/, which also swallowed ~230 KB of hand-written source --
# the board top, the .cob, the constraints and the build scripts -- despite
# the comment above saying "not source". Git cannot re-include a path under
# an excluded directory, so a negation would not have worked; the rules have
# to name the build dirs directly.
/fpga/haps-sx/build/
/fpga/haps-sx-pc/build/
# Tool droppings written into $PWD rather than work/. Genus, Innovus and IC
# Validator all log relative to the directory they were LAUNCHED from, so a
# one-off probe (`genus -batch -command get_db`, `icv`, or an ad-hoc
# `innovus -files experiment.tcl`) lands them wherever it was started, which
# the ASIC/*/{logs,work} rules do not reach.
#
# These rules are deliberately NOT anchored with a leading slash. They started
# out as /genus.log* etc., which only ever matched the repo root -- and then a
# scratch run in ASIC/genus-innovus/holdexp/ dropped 700 KB of innovus.log*,
# innovus.logv* and innovus.cmd* into a directory that also holds hand-written
# .tcl, so neither the anchored rules nor a whole-directory ignore would do.
# Unanchored, they follow the tool to whatever directory it was invoked in.
#
# `log*` covers three families in one glob and is meant to: the plain log, the
# VERBOSE log Innovus writes alongside it (innovus.logv -- this is a distinct
# file, not a typo for .log), and the rotation suffixes both take on re-runs
# (innovus.log1, innovus.logv2, innovus.cmd2, genus.log1, ...).
genus.log*
genus.cmd*
innovus.log*
innovus.cmd*
# Anchored, unlike the above: a stray stderr capture at the root is a dropping,
# but "err.txt" is an ordinary name for a test fixture somewhere in verif/.
/err.txt
.icv.*
# Baseline P&R snapshots. scripts/ci/asic_stage_report.sh takes BASELINE_DIR and
# diffs the current run against one of these; each is a full copy of
# logs/ outputs/ reports/ work/ and runs to ~2.7 GB. The existing
# ASIC/*/{logs,outputs,reports,work} rules sit one directory level too high to
# match them, since the copy nests them under ASIC/*/baseline_<date>/.
ASIC/*/baseline_*/
